  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe forecast becomes a bit more muddy heading into the end of this \nweek and through the weekend. Currently the NHC shows a TC likely \nforming over the E Pacific, south of the tip of the Baja Del Norte \nwithin the next 24-48 hrs. Most forecast models show the storm \nitself curving out into open waters, however there is good evidence \nthat at least some tropical moisture will get pulled up into Mexico \nand making it as far north as southern AZ as early as Thursday.\nRoughly 90% of the GEFS and EPS members show PWATs increasing from \n1.5\" on Thursday to around 1.8-2.0\" over portions of southcentral \nand southwest AZ by Friday or Saturday. Given this likely increase \nin moisture, NBM PoPs have increased to around 40-50% with the \ninitial moisture push late Thursday into Friday. Thereafter, PoPs \nremain quite low in a 20-30% range through the weekend which is \nmost likely due to uncertainty in timing and coverage of rainfall \nwithin the deterministic model output. \n\nAlthough there will be adequate moisture return, the positioning \nof the ridge/trough features over the west will play a key role in\nhow much rainfall may actually materialize. If the ridge \nstrengthens too much and retrogrades wwd, then the focus for \nheavier rainfall may be much further west into SoCal and north \ninto UT and CO as indicated by the latest ECMWF and GFS runs. It \nwill likely be at least 2 or 3 more days until we have a good idea\non what will happen late this week, and any forecast that does \nnot show a range of possible outcomes should be disregarded at \nthis time. \n\nUncertainty in the temperature forecast by this weekend is also \napparent as indicated by large (8-10 degree) spreads within the \nNBM interquartile range. Once our region does begin to draw in the\nPacific moisture, temperatures will likely dip down to around 3-5\ndegrees below normal by the end of this week. However, as ridging\naloft attempts to build over the area this weekend, temperatures \nmay warm closer to normal by Sunday or Monday.",
